WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.330
so U S income inequality is hit the highest
level in 50 years. We have just learned, and

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:10.820
this is measured using something called the
Guinea index. G I. N I.

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:16.720
Let's talk about how the metric works. This
is a measure of distribution of income or

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.480
wealth. You can have an income distribution,
a measured with Guinea or wealth distribution

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.109
and it's across income percentiles. So it
goes from zero to one. Zero would be there

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:33.531
is perfect equality if everyone has the same
income, the Guinea coefficient is zero. If

00:00:33.531 --> 00:00:39.550
everyone has the same wealth, the Guinea coefficient
is zero. One would be perfect. Inequality,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.800
meaning if it, you have a country and one
person has all the income or all the wealth,

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.610
the Guinea coefficients for income and wealth
respectively, it would be one and you can

00:00:50.610 --> 00:01:00.239
track it over time. So in the United States
in 2017 it was 0.482 and it went up in 2018

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:07.499
to 0.485. It's an increase of about half a
percent. This is a very big calculation. So

00:01:07.499 --> 00:01:11.829
it moves really, really slowly and then you
can break it down in more detail.

00:01:11.829 --> 00:01:18.340
If you want. You could look at, let's look
at a change in the, uh, inequality, uh, by

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.659
gender or look at it by the States. People
live in blue States versus red States, or

00:01:23.659 --> 00:01:29.310
you could look at it by race or by age or
whatever you want. Wealth inequality went

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:35.139
up in quite a few Heartland States, which
is interesting because Donald Trump's trade

00:01:35.139 --> 00:01:41.929
policy, uh, is potentially to blame. They're
actually impacting inequality, uh, both income

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:46.889
and wealth. And some of those Heartland States,
States on the East coast and the West coast

00:01:46.889 --> 00:01:52.569
still have higher inequality overall. And
that's really not surprising since you have

00:01:52.569 --> 00:01:58.569
more wealthy people on the coasts and the
most equal States, interestingly enough, are

00:01:58.569 --> 00:02:05.279
Utah, Alaska, um, and the Dakotas and maybe
Iowa as well, is on that list. And the right

00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:10.629
loves to look at that and say, look, you've
got all these liberals who talk about equality

00:02:10.629 --> 00:02:15.530
and they say they want equality, but it's
the red States that actually have the most

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:16.530
equality.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:22.620
And of course this is one of those very low
energy interpretations. It's not a very, uh,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:28.519
a high thinking interpretation because the
reason that a lot of red States have low inequality

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:33.890
is that you just have way more people that
are not doing well. And the truth is that

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:39.900
the degree of inequality matters. There's
lots of externalities as well, crime and others,

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.450
but the reason for inequality is also really
important. So if you want to get an understanding

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.280
of this, if you want to understand inequality,
number one, there's a lot of good books that

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.920
I could recommend that you should read, but
when you're looking at something like the

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:58.920
Guinea coefficient, you need to often look
qualitatively, and if I'm to pick my battles,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:06.170
I have way less of a problem with an entrepreneur
who becomes wealthy by creating something

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:11.560
compared to someone who inherits a ton of
money and then uses the money to buy influence

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:16.530
and to influence legislation to allow them
to get even wealthier.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:22.010
Now I want to be clear, we can argue all inequality
is a problem, whether it's inherited or whether

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:26.819
it's the new rich of entrepreneurship and
the tech world, but when you break it down,

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:32.220
there are different categories and it's important
that on the left, if we want to fight against

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:38.129
inequality, we should understand the sources
of it and make sure that we're dealing with

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:44.099
the most serious problems first. Now, that
being said, we have a system that encourages

00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:50.780
even the entrepreneurs who become wealthy
through the business they've created to then

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:55.430
get into the influence pedaling and to become
one of the people that is indistinguishable

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:59.549
from those who have just inherited money and
use that money to peddle influence and to

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:04.889
change legislation for their benefit. And
then of course, you also have entrepreneurs

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:12.069
who are getting wealthy thanks to the tax
sheltering laws and the other stuff that the

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:15.540
legacy influence peddlers were able to get
past.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.729
So it's not a clean cut the way I might be
suggesting, but the point is we should be

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:27.560
able to look at this issue more qualitatively
and really determine what is most important

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:32.509
to be addressing right away. Uh, but the reality
is that if this continues to go in the same

00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:37.410
direction of growing inequality, it's going
to cause more social unrest. It's going to

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:44.060
cause more crime. It's ultimately going to
precipitate an increasingly unsustainable

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:49.220
system. Now the solution to the problem is
different. Depending on your political ideology.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:54.419
You might have someone who is an actual socialist.
I'm not talking about someone who wants government

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:59.180
health care, but you might have an actual
socialist who says, the only solution is to

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:04.520
totally socialize the means of production,
socialized property. And that's the way that

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:08.590
we're going to fix this problem. Social Democrats
would have a different point of view, which

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:15.650
is some degree of inequality is okay, but
what we want to do is put some limits at the

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:22.949
top and use those to raise the floor so that
we are constricting inequality to a more narrow

00:05:22.949 --> 00:05:29.570
band such that people aren't getting left
behind such that the riches that come to some,

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:38.340
the few from the system that we have are by
statute, by law being reinvested to better

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:40.020
circumstances for everybody.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.640
That would be a socially democratic approach.
And that's really at the heart of a lot of

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.630
the debate that is happening within the left
right now. Uh, certainly the right has a completely

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:52.240
different point of view with so-called free
markets and all of the other things that they

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.060
say are really great, which have completely
failed us over the last 150 years in this

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:02.250
country. But the data, once again, income
inequality increasing in the United States

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:03.050
from 2017 to 2018

